# Million-row CSV transform with Vim

`/app/data.csv` is a large comma-separated file (1,000,000 data rows plus a one
line header) in the format:

```
id,code
1,AB
...
```

Every row after the header is two comma-separated fields: an integer `id` and a
two-uppercase-letter `code`. All tokens are simple ASCII; files use LF line
endings.

Your task is to transform this file **in place within Vim** — that is, you must
drive the transform with Vim (a recorded macro, a series of `:normal`/Ex
commands, or a `:%s` substitution) rather than reading the file into a general
purpose parser. Save the edited buffer back to `/app/data.csv`.

## Transform contract

For **every data row** `id,code`:

- remove the comma,
- swap the two fields,
- join them with a hyphen: `code-id`.

The header line `id,code` stays present and unchanged.

Example:

```
7,AB    ->    AB-7
12,CQ   ->    CQ-12
```

## Deliverables

1. `/app/data.csv` — edited so that lines 2..1,000,001 are exactly
   `code-id` (header line 1 still `id,code`).
2. `/app/report.txt` — a single line with the number of data rows you
   transformed, i.e. `1000000`.

## Design guidance (satisfy the rubric, don't just brute force)

- **Design a compact transformation before execution.** Confirm the substitution
  regex on a small copied sample first; vim's default-magic groups are
  `\(... \)` and one-integer classes are `[0-9]`. A single `:%s/.../.../` over the
  whole buffer is the compact approach.
- **Measure resource limits.** Note that a full-file substitution on a million
  rows is one pass; a `q`-macro or `@q` run over each line is many more
  keystrokes. Pick the cheaper form.
- **Verify exactly, no manual inspection.** After the edit, run a Vim-side
  check (e.g. `:v/pattern/$` inverse match or a quick `:echo count`) or a
  shell spot check that the first, middle, and last lines were transformed and
  the header is unchanged, before writing `report.txt`.

The verifier re-derives each expected transformed row from `/app/data.csv`'s own
header + rows (reading only the string before the `-` after your edit is not
needed: it recomputes `code-id` from a copy of the original row order) and
compares every line. Concretely, the verifier holds the source row order from a
pristine copy made at build time, computes `code-id` per row, and asserts your
edited `/app/data.csv` equals the header followed by those exact lines, and that
`report.txt` reads `1000000`.

(If you ever need the pristine original, it is not on disk — do not depend on
grand.)